1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:03,673
A sal tree near Hetauda, thirty metres tall.

2
00:00:03,673 --> 00:00:09,673
Every leaf at the top is full of water, and all of it came from the soil, thirty metres below.

3
00:00:09,900 --> 00:00:13,092
Something lifted it, ten storeys, every day.

4
00:00:13,092 --> 00:00:16,375
And the tree has no heart, no pump, no muscle.

5
00:00:16,667 --> 00:00:22,215
Yet a suction pump on a well cannot lift water more than about ten metres.

6
00:00:22,215 --> 00:00:24,526
Pull harder and the water column breaks.

7
00:00:25,200 --> 00:00:27,936
The sal does three times that, silently.

8
00:00:27,936 --> 00:00:28,665
How?

9
00:00:28,665 --> 00:00:33,665
The surprising answer, by the end of this lesson: the water is not pushed up.

10
00:00:33,665 --> 00:00:34,882
It is pulled from above.

11
00:00:40,367 --> 00:00:45,525
Welcome to Class 12 Biology, Unit 2: Plant Physiology.

12
00:00:45,525 --> 00:00:47,335
Unit 1 built the plumbing.

13
00:00:47,335 --> 00:00:48,220
Now we switch it on.

14
00:00:50,600 --> 00:00:57,598
Five stops: water relations, photosynthesis, respiration, plant hormones, and growth and movement.

15
00:01:01,933 --> 00:01:03,966
Drop dye into still water.

16
00:01:03,966 --> 00:01:09,539
Its particles jiggle with heat energy and spread from crowded to scarce.

17
00:01:09,539 --> 00:01:10,538
That is diffusion.

18
00:01:10,767 --> 00:01:14,453
No membrane, no energy, no living cell needed.

19
00:01:14,453 --> 00:01:20,224
It is fast over micrometres but hopeless over metres, which is why plants need xylem.

20
00:01:21,433 --> 00:01:25,640
Soak a dry pea overnight and it swells to twice its size.

21
00:01:25,640 --> 00:01:31,587
That is imbibition: water adsorbed by hydrophilic colloids, the first step of germination.

22
00:01:31,800 --> 00:01:38,084
Protein imbibes most, then starch, then cellulose, so a pea swells more than a wheat grain.

23
00:01:39,700 --> 00:01:44,571
Osmosis is diffusion through a selectively permeable membrane.

24
00:01:44,571 --> 00:01:47,178
Water passes through it; sugar does not.

25
00:01:47,400 --> 00:01:54,634
More water crosses into the sugar side than comes back, so there is a net flow, and the solution column rises.

26
00:01:54,933 --> 00:01:59,788
The pressure needed to stop that flow is the osmotic pressure of the solution.

27
00:02:02,000 --> 00:02:03,408
Exam tip.

28
00:02:03,408 --> 00:02:09,515
Don't define osmosis as dilute to concentrated; that fails once pressure acts.

29
00:02:09,515 --> 00:02:13,170
Say: water moves from higher to lower water potential.

30
00:02:13,967 --> 00:02:18,552
Water potential, psi, measures the free energy of water.

31
00:02:18,552 --> 00:02:22,818
Pure water at atmospheric pressure is the reference: zero.

32
00:02:23,033 --> 00:02:28,842
Solutes and surfaces lower it, so every solution and cell is negative.

33
00:02:28,842 --> 00:02:32,306
Water always moves from less negative to more negative.

34
00:02:33,533 --> 00:02:35,774
For a cell, two terms matter.

35
00:02:35,774 --> 00:02:39,654
Psi s, the solute potential, is negative.

36
00:02:39,654 --> 00:02:46,132
Psi p, the pressure potential, is positive in a turgid cell, and negative in the xylem.

37
00:02:46,333 --> 00:02:51,123
So the working equation is psi w equals psi s plus psi p.

38
00:02:52,333 --> 00:02:58,506
A cell with psi s of minus zero point nine sits in a bath at minus zero point one.

39
00:02:58,506 --> 00:02:59,356
Water flows in.

40
00:02:59,567 --> 00:03:03,097
The protoplast swells and the wall pushes back.

41
00:03:03,097 --> 00:03:06,699
Psi p climbs, and psi w climbs with it.

42
00:03:06,967 --> 00:03:13,687
At psi p of plus zero point eight, psi w is minus zero point one, equal to the bath.

43
00:03:13,687 --> 00:03:16,193
Net flow stops: the cell is turgid.

44
00:03:17,767 --> 00:03:18,993
A worked example.

45
00:03:18,993 --> 00:03:22,118
One molar sucrose at twenty-five degrees.

46
00:03:22,118 --> 00:03:27,239
Find its solute potential, and the fate of a cell at minus one point two.

47
00:03:27,467 --> 00:03:30,486
Use psi s equals minus i C R T.

48
00:03:30,700 --> 00:03:33,422
Sucrose doesn't ionise, so i is one.

49
00:03:33,633 --> 00:03:37,963
T is twenty-five plus two seventy-three: two ninety-eight kelvin.

50
00:03:38,167 --> 00:03:44,406
R is zero point zero zero eight three one, in litre megapascal per mole per kelvin.

51
00:03:44,633 --> 00:03:48,496
Psi s is minus two point four eight megapascal.

52
00:03:48,700 --> 00:03:54,701
The solution is more negative than the cell, so water leaves the cell and it plasmolyses.

53
00:03:55,933 --> 00:03:59,597
Now a turgid cell, psi p plus zero point eight.

54
00:03:59,800 --> 00:04:01,585
Put it in that sucrose.

55
00:04:01,585 --> 00:04:06,481
Water leaves, pressure falls, and the protoplast just starts to leave the wall.

56
00:04:06,481 --> 00:04:10,297
Psi p is zero: incipient plasmolysis.

57
00:04:10,567 --> 00:04:15,660
More water leaves and the protoplast shrinks away: plasmolysed.

58
00:04:15,660 --> 00:04:20,613
The gap fills with sugar solution, because the wall is freely permeable.

59
00:04:21,300 --> 00:04:25,078
Back in water it recovers: deplasmolysis.

60
00:04:25,078 --> 00:04:26,816
Only a living cell can do this.

61
00:04:28,500 --> 00:04:34,074
Water enters a root through root hairs, whose sap is more negative than the soil water.

62
00:04:34,300 --> 00:04:43,521
It crosses the cortex through cell walls, the apoplast, or from cytoplasm to cytoplasm through plasmodesmata, the symplast.

63
00:04:43,733 --> 00:04:49,594
At the endodermis, the Casparian strip of lignin and suberin blocks the wall route.

64
00:04:49,594 --> 00:04:51,958
Everything must cross a living membrane.

65
00:04:53,567 --> 00:04:57,410
The endodermis also pumps ions into the xylem.

66
00:04:57,410 --> 00:05:00,994
Water follows by osmosis, creating root pressure.

67
00:05:02,600 --> 00:05:13,344
But root pressure cannot lift sap up a tree: it is far too small, it vanishes at midday, many conifers lack it, and sap still rises in killed stems.

68
00:05:13,567 --> 00:05:18,115
Its real job is refilling air-blocked vessels at night, and guttation.

69
00:05:19,333 --> 00:05:20,559
Back to the sal.

70
00:05:20,559 --> 00:05:24,022
Its xylem holds an unbroken thread of water.

71
00:05:24,022 --> 00:05:29,086
The cohesion tension theory of Dixon and Joly explains it with three pillars.

72
00:05:29,367 --> 00:05:31,556
One: transpiration pull.

73
00:05:31,556 --> 00:05:38,151
Water evaporates from leaf cell walls and escapes through stomata, tugging on the water behind it.

74
00:05:38,433 --> 00:05:43,461
So xylem water is under tension: its pressure potential is negative.

75
00:05:43,461 --> 00:05:46,829
The energy comes from the sun, not from metabolism.

76
00:05:47,100 --> 00:05:48,677
Two: cohesion.

77
00:05:48,677 --> 00:05:53,371
Hydrogen bonds hold water molecules together, so the column doesn't snap.

78
00:05:53,667 --> 00:05:55,309
Three: adhesion.

79
00:05:55,309 --> 00:05:57,353
Water clings to the vessel walls.

80
00:05:58,633 --> 00:06:06,447
Water potential falls all the way: soil, root, trunk, leaf, and then minus ninety-three in dry air.

81
00:06:06,447 --> 00:06:08,703
That last huge step does the pulling.

82
00:06:09,367 --> 00:06:11,608
So the sal has no pump at the bottom.

83
00:06:11,608 --> 00:06:15,339
The sun pulls from the top, and the water column is the rope.

84
00:06:15,933 --> 00:06:22,080
Living-pump theories died when Strasburger poisoned oak trunks and sap still rose.

85
00:06:22,080 --> 00:06:25,691
Capillarity lifts water only about sixty centimetres.

86
00:06:28,567 --> 00:06:29,780
Pause and think.

87
00:06:29,780 --> 00:06:33,117
What is the sign of pressure potential in xylem at midday?

88
00:06:40,000 --> 00:06:43,114
Negative: the column is under tension.

89
00:06:43,114 --> 00:06:48,284
Positive is the trap; that describes a turgid cell or root pressure at night.

90
00:06:49,067 --> 00:06:57,558
Most absorbed water is lost as vapour, mostly through stomata: transpiration, the price of letting carbon dioxide in.

91
00:06:57,558 --> 00:07:04,275
Each stoma is a pore between two kidney-shaped guard cells, which have chloroplasts and a thick inner wall.

92
00:07:04,500 --> 00:07:10,218
Cellulose microfibrils hoop around each guard cell: radial micellation.

93
00:07:10,218 --> 00:07:15,325
So swelling makes the cells longer, not fatter, and they bow apart.

94
00:07:16,533 --> 00:07:18,891
Blue light switches on a proton pump.

95
00:07:18,891 --> 00:07:23,522
Protons leave the guard cells, and the inside turns negative.

96
00:07:23,733 --> 00:07:28,671
Potassium ions flood in, with chloride and malate to balance the charge.

97
00:07:28,900 --> 00:07:35,109
Psi s drops, water rushes in, the cells bow apart, and the pore opens.

98
00:07:35,333 --> 00:07:40,454
Under water stress, A B A makes them lose potassium, and the pore closes.

99
00:07:42,067 --> 00:07:45,454
Note: the old starch sugar theory is obsolete.

100
00:07:45,454 --> 00:07:48,965
Onion guard cells have no starch, yet work perfectly.

101
00:07:50,167 --> 00:07:54,413
On humid mornings, drops form at leaf tips and margins.

102
00:07:54,413 --> 00:07:59,797
This is guttation: liquid sap pushed out through hydathodes by root pressure.

103
00:08:00,000 --> 00:08:06,409
It happens when root pressure is high and transpiration low: at night, in moist soil and humid air.

104
00:08:08,033 --> 00:08:16,464
Unlike dew, guttation water comes from inside the plant and leaves a salty crust; dew is pure, condensed from air.

